@database "mail"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: mail.guide 1.0 (07.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Mail"
@{h1}Mail

Sends a plain-text mail over SMTP. An agent or a workflow calls one tool
with a subject and a text, for example to tell you that a job has
finished, what it made and where it lies, or why it failed. The mail goes
only to the recipients you name in the server entry: the model can pick
some of them, but it cannot write to any other address, not even when a
web page it read tells it to.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The send tool  " link tool}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node tool "The send tool"
@{h1}The send tool

@{tt}<instance>_send@{utt} sends one mail to some or all of the configured
recipients. With the instance name @{tt}mail@{utt} the tool is
@{tt}mail_send@{utt}.

@{h2}Parameters

@{table}
Name | Meaning
subject | required, one line of at most 200 characters; no line break of any kind, so nothing can be smuggled into the headers
body | required, plain text of at most 100000 characters, sent as UTF-8; text that UTF-8 cannot hold (a lone surrogate) is refused
to | optional, a list of addresses (a single string works too); each must be one of the configured recipients, compared without case. Left out or empty: all of them
@{body}

Every check runs before a connection is opened. The mail carries the
configured sender in From, the chosen recipients in To, the date and a
message id.

@{h2}Answer

@{code}
{"status": "success", "sent_to": ["me@example.org"]}
@{body}

@{tt}sent_to@{utt} names the recipients as they stand in the configuration.
When the SMTP server took the mail but refused some recipients, they are
listed in @{tt}refused@{utt} beside the others. A mail that did not go out
answers @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt}.

@{h2}Example

@{code}
mail_send(subject="Report ready", body="The weekly report is in data/reports/week41.md")
@{body}
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tool

The description and parameter texts come from @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}:
"Send a plain-text mail to the configured recipients. Returns the
addresses it went to." The description names no address; the answer lists
the ones the mail went to.

Errors say what to change:

@{bullet}"no SMTP server configured: set host in the mail server entry"
@{bullet}"security: one of starttls, ssl, none in the mail server entry, not '...'"
@{bullet}"no sender address configured: set sender (or username) in the mail server entry"
@{bullet}"no recipients configured: set recipients in the mail server entry"
@{bullet}"subject is required: one line of at most 200 characters"
@{bullet}"body is required: plain text of at most 100000 characters"
@{bullet}"to: only configured recipients can be written to; leave it out to write to all of them"
@{bullet}"the SMTP server refused the login (535): check username and password"
@{bullet}"the SMTP server refused every recipient: ..."
@{bullet}"mail not sent: SMTP sends the username, the password and the
addresses as ASCII" when one of them holds other characters; the
password is never repeated in an answer.
@{bullet}"mail not sent: <error type>: <what happened>" when the server cannot be
reached, the TLS handshake fails, the connection times out or the server
answers otherwise.

The chat's status line ends with "Mail to <n> recipient(s): <subject>".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The answer is an ordinary tool result of one line. It is appended, and
nothing earlier changes.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Plain text only: no HTML and no attachments. A file is named by its
path in the text.
@{bullet}One attempt: a server that is down is not tried again. The caller
decides whether to send once more.
@{bullet}Recipients are bare addresses. A display name ("Name <a\@b.org>")
in the recipient list is not supported.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

@{h2}The server entry

The plugin is off in the default configuration (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}).
Switch it on where you keep your own settings, for example in
@{tt}config/local.yaml@{utt}, and name your SMTP server and the
recipients:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    mail:
      type: mail
      enabled: true
      host: smtp.example.org
      username: bot@example.org
      password: "${MAIL_PASSWORD}"
      recipients: [me@example.org]
@{body}

Put the password into @{tt}config/secrets.env@{utt} as
@{tt}MAIL_PASSWORD=...@{utt}; the configuration fills in
@{tt}${MAIL_PASSWORD}@{utt} from there. For Gmail, the host is
@{tt}smtp.gmail.com@{utt} and the password an app password (Google
account, Security, App passwords; it needs two-step verification).

@{h2}The allowlist

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+mail/*"
@{body}

A workflow reaches the tool through its runner agent, whose allowlist
needs the same line.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
host | (none) | the SMTP server; without it every call answers with an error
security | starttls | starttls (plain connection, then TLS), ssl (TLS from the start) or none; with none a login sends the password unencrypted, so use it only for a server on your own machine or network
port | 587, 465 for ssl, 25 for none | the server's port
username | (none) | the login; empty means no login
password | (none) | the login's password, best "${MAIL_PASSWORD}"
sender | the username | the From address, also "Name <address>"
recipients | (none) | the addresses the tool may write to: a list, or one string with commas
timeout_s | 30 | seconds for the connection and each answer of the server
@{body}

The TLS connection checks the server's certificate against the system's
trusted authorities. The plugin keeps no data.
@endnode
